    <glyph id="CRY1" class="macromolecule" compartmentRef="nucleus">
      <label text="Cryptochrome 1" />
      <bbox x="0.00" y="0.00" w="126.95" h="47.44" />
      <glyph id="CRY1__u0" class="unit of information">
        <label text="FAD radical pair" />
        <bbox x="6.00" y="-9.00" w="112.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <extension>
        <qbo:annotation xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0" entity="QBO:CRY1" evidenceTier="T1" confidence="verified">
          <qbo:quantumClass>radical_pair_acceptor</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>flavin_semiquinone</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>triplet_yield_sensitive</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:nucleus>1H</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:nucleus>14N</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:cofactor>CHEBI:57692</qbo:cofactor>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT4G08920</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="go_component">GO:0005634</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:evidence ref="Ahmad2007" doi="10.1007/s00425-006-0383-0" direction="altered" fieldRegime="static_MF">Magnetic intensity affects cryptochrome-dependent responses in Arabidopsis thaliana.
</qbo:evidence>
          <qbo:evidence ref="Solovyov2007" doi="10.1529/biophysj.106.097139" direction="altered" fieldRegime="static_MF">Theoretical analysis of Arabidopsis cryptochrome-1 shows magnetic fields could alter activation yields through radical-pair spin dynamics.
</qbo:evidence>
          <qbo:evidence ref="Harris2009" doi="10.1098/rsif.2008.0519" direction="no_effect" fieldRegime="static_MF">Under blinded conditions, cryptochrome-dependent magnetic-field effects in Arabidopsis were NOT reproduced.
</qbo:evidence>
          <qbo:evidence ref="Pooam2019" doi="10.1007/s00425-018-3002-y" direction="altered" fieldRegime="static_MF">Magnetic sensitivity mediated by Arabidopsis cryptochrome occurs during flavin reoxidation in the dark, not during photoreduction.
</qbo:evidence>
          <qbo:evidence ref="Xu2014" doi="10.1016/j.asr.2014.01.033" direction="altered" fieldRegime="NNMF">Blue-light-dependent phosphorylation of cryptochromes is affected by magnetic fields in Arabidopsis.
</qbo:evidence>
          <qbo:rationale>The reference case for the radical-pair mechanism in biology, and the entity from which the review's whole argument is generalised outward.
</qbo:rationale>
          <qbo:caveat>Direction of effect is not consistent across the literature, and one blinded replication attempt failed (see Harris2009 below). Treat the magnitude as unsettled even though the mechanism is well characterised.
</qbo:caveat>
        </qbo:annotation>
      </extension>
    </glyph>
    <glyph id="CRY2" class="macromolecule" compartmentRef="nucleus">
      <label text="Cryptochrome 2" />
      <bbox x="160.95" y="0.00" w="126.84" h="47.44" />
      <glyph id="CRY2__u0" class="unit of information">
        <label text="photoperiod" />
        <bbox x="166.95" y="-9.00" w="77.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
      <extension>
        <qbo:annotation xmlns:qbo="https://dr-richard-barker.github.io/quantum-biology-atlas/qbo/1.0" entity="QBO:CRY2" evidenceTier="T2" confidence="reported">
          <qbo:quantumClass>radical_pair_acceptor</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>flavin_semiquinone</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:quantumClass>triplet_yield_sensitive</qbo:quantumClass>
          <qbo:nucleus>1H</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:nucleus>14N</qbo:nucleus>
          <qbo:cofactor>CHEBI:57692</qbo:cofactor>
          <qbo:xref db="agi">AT1G04400</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:xref db="go_component">GO:0005634</qbo:xref>
          <qbo:evidence ref="Xu2012" doi="10.1016/j.asr.2011.12.004" direction="altered" fieldRegime="NNMF">A near-null magnetic field affects cryptochrome-related hypocotyl growth and flowering in Arabidopsis.
</qbo:evidence>
          <qbo:evidence ref="Agliassa2018b" doi="10.1016/j.jphotobiol.2018.05.027" direction="altered" fieldRegime="NNMF">Geomagnetic field variation impacts cryptochrome and phytochrome signalling.
</qbo:evidence>
          <qbo:rationale>Same FAD/tryptophan-triad chemistry as CRY1. CRY2's route to flowering time is the mechanistic link between the photoreceptor evidence and the most reproducible NNMF phenotype in the literature — delayed flowering.
</qbo:rationale>
        </qbo:annotation>
      </extension>
    </glyph>
    <glyph id="PHYB" class="macromolecule" compartmentRef="nucleus">
      <label text="Phytochrome B" />
      <bbox x="321.79" y="0.00" w="120.73" h="47.44" />
      <glyph id="PHYB__u0" class="unit of information">
        <label text="no spin route" />
        <bbox x="327.79" y="-9.00" w="91.00" h="16.00" />
      </glyph>
